{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment3.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Second `DefaultAuthService` constructed even when one is injected**

`main()` already builds and injects the production `DefaultAuthService`, but `initState`
unconditionally constructs another instance that is dead in production (the
`_service` getter prefers `widget.authService`). Only fall back when the injected
service is absent:

```dart
@override
void initState() {
  super.initState();
  _authService = widget.authService ??
      DefaultAuthService(
        gateway: DefaultAuthGateway(),
        sessionStore: SessionStore(),
      );
  _session = _service.restoreSession();
  _resolveAppleAvailability();
}
```

This keeps a single production wiring path (either inject in `main()` or default in the
widget, not both).
"}

{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment4.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: Two consecutive `setState` calls can be one**

```dart
setState(() => _errorMessage = null);
setState(() => _busy = true);
```

Both mutate state before the same frame is scheduled; merge them:

```dart
setState(() {
  _errorMessage = null;
  _busy = true;
});
```
"}

{"path":"outputs/pr_review_comments/comment5.md","content":"💡 **SUGGESTION: `_signOut` has no error handling or in-flight guard**

```dart
Future<void> _signOut() async {
  await authService.signOut();
  onSignedOut?.call();
}
```

If `signOut()` throws (e.g. the store/plugin call fails), the future rejects
unhandled: no feedback, user stays on Home. Also, a double-tap runs it twice.
Consider guarding and reporting:

```dart
bool _signingOut = false;

Future<void> _signOut() async {
  if (_signingOut) return;
  _signingOut = true;
  try {
    await authService.signOut();
    onSignedOut?.call();
  } catch (_) {
    if (context.mounted) {
      ScaffoldMessenger.of(context).showSnackBar(
        const SnackBar(content: Text('Sign out failed. Please try again.')),
      );
    }
  } finally {
    _signingOut = false;
  }
}
```

Low risk today (`DefaultAuthGateway.signOut` swallows Google errors), but the UI layer
should not rely on every gateway implementation being failure-free.
"}

